ABSTRACT
The J-integral, which is crucial to the nonlinear fracture analysis within the framework of continuum fracture mechanics, fails at the atomic scale due to the atomic discreteness emerging with decreasing size. In this paper, a novel atomic J-integral calculation method is proposed for atomically sharp cracks by developing a new integral scheme to describe the local displacement gradient and stress field for discrete models. The applicability of the atomic J-integral is verified by comparing with traditional FEM and stress-based method, and its pathindependency is elucidated by evaluating the geometric nonlinearity at the atomic scale. Furthermore, it is corroborated that the atomic J-integral method is dimension-independent even at the ultra-small scale below the dimensional limit of continuum mechanics.
1. Introduction
How to determine the growth of preexisting cracks in materials is the paramount topic in fracture mechanics. Generally, the cracking in brittle materials is dominated by linear elasticity at the macroscale, since the nonlinear deformation is highly localized in the vicinity of the crack tip. As the material size shrinks down to the nanoscale, however, such atomic-level nonlinearity plays a crucial role in the fracture process and should be fully considered [31]. Eshelby’s seminal work [5] about the configuration force proposed a conservative integral as a mathematical tool, until Rice [26] originally introduced the J-integral concept and clarified its physical significance. For nonlinear fracture mechanics (NLFM), the J-integral evaluates the energetic force driving the propagation of an existing crack and the intensity of the crack tip fields in nonlinear elastic continuum [32,33]. The nonlinear elastic fracture refers to the pure brittle fracture of the material without any plastic behavior. The path independence also provides great convenience for solving the J-integral by numerical simulations, considering that the measurement of the energy released at the crack tip is difficult. As the critical value JC refers to the fracture toughness, the J-integral criterion possesses a rigorous theoretical basis and physical significance. Since the J-integral was constructed within the framework of
continuum fracture mechanics (CFM), it remains an open question whether the J-integral works for cracking issues in nano-sized structures or not. When the crack dimension decreases to the nanoscale, the singular stress field, which intrinsically dominates the fracture in the theory of CFM, will shrink down to a few nanometers. In such cases, only an extremely smaller number of atoms exist near the crack tip, compared with that in the macroscale materials. Therefore, the postulate that a large number of atoms exist in the vicinity of the crack tip to regard the area as a continuum is no longer valid at the atomic scale. And this leads to the breakdown of CFM [46,30,9,6,7,35,43]. According to a series of studies about the failure of continuum theories at the nanoscale, various criteria within the framework of CFM, including the Griffith energy release rate (ERR) criterion [30], J-integral criterion [31] and strength criterion [9,15], will break down when describing the brittle elastic fracture at the nanoscale. To break through the lower dimensional limit of CFM, some attempts have been made by applying the atomic fracture mechanics (AFM). A representative example is the atomic ERR method, which characterizes the first bond breaking at the crack tip [30,9,10,31,15]. Nevertheless, it is still not easy for the atomic ERR method to deal with multiscale fracture problems, e.g., an extremely small (nano-sized) crack in a large (macro-sized) component. Because the atomic ERR is calculated by counting the change of strain energy of the whole structure. Considering
