 decreases below the dimensional limit of CFM. In this paper, an AFM-based J-integral (JAFM) concept is proposed to solve the nonlinear fracture of nanostructures. We avoid calculating the displacement gradients of atoms by meshing the atomic structure with quadrilateral isoparametric elements and adding Gaussian points inside the elements. The line integral along the crack face is considered in the Jintegral. The accuracy and path-independency are verified by a series of
numerical examples based on the graphene nanoribbons (GNRs). Furthermore, we demonstrate the breakdown of the conventional J-integral based on NLFM and give the lower dimensional limit at the atomic scale. The dimensional-independency of JAFM introduced in this paper is discussed by comparing it with the CFM method.
