3. Material and models
In this work, we take the graphene nanoribbons (GNRs) as a representative of 2D brittle materials with periodic hexagonal honeycomb structure [38,25,14,44,22,37]. A large number of theoretical studies on the brittle fracture of graphene have been done and CFM has been verified to be applicable in characterizing the fracture behavior of oneatom-thick GNRs [41,40,46,13,29,36,4,47,2].
3.1. Atomistic model
The calculation of JAFM is based on the molecular statics (MS) simulation of quasi-static tensile tests for cracked GNRs, using the Largescale Atomic/Molecular Massively Parallel Simulator (LAMMPS) [24]. The AIREBO potential (Adaptive Intermolecular Reactive Empirical
Bond Order Potential) [34]is used to characterize the sp2 bond between
carbon atoms. The influence of kinetic energy is not considered during the whole process. A single-edge-cracked specimen is introduced and stretched along the armchair direction, as shown in Fig. 2a. The geometric relationship of length H, width W, and crack length a is H = 2W, W = 3a. The thickness of monolayer graphene is regarded as 0.335 nm [23], which is the average interlayer distance of graphite. The lattice constant is 0.14 nm. Dimension along the length (armchair) direction is fixed by the periodic boundary condition. Considering that the unit cells of GNRs possess a hexagonal honeycomb structure, it is difficult to directly build an isoparametric element with six atoms as nodes. Thus, the graphene hexagonal cell is divided into two quadrilateral isoparametric elements instead, as shown in Fig. 2b. Nine Gaussian points are set for each element.
3.2. FEM model
The finite element analysis is performed in the following analysis to calculate the traditional J-integral as a comparison with JAFM for the discrete atomic system. The FEM model is given in Fig. 2c. The dimensions of the FEM model are the same as those of MS simulation. The bottom boundary is fixed, and the critical displacement d obtained from atomic simulation is applied to the upper boundary. Ramberg-Osgood equation is assumed to describe the nonlinear elastic behavior in the uniaxial tension tests for GNRs, i.e.,
