4. Results and discussion
The global stress–strain (σglobal-εglobal) relation of the pristine GNR demonstrates that graphene possesses eminent nonlinear elasticity at the tensile strain larger than 0.06 during the uniaxial tensile test, as shown in Fig. 3a. For cracked nano-sized GNRs, the global mechanical responses (σglobal-εglobal relations) of specimens with different sizes (a = 0.7 ∼ 14.0 nm) are shown in Fig. 3b. It can be observed that the cracked GNRs can withstand the greater deformation at fracture when the specimen size decreases. Since the critical εglobal is always less than 0.06, the nonlinearity of the entire specimen is attributed to the local nonlinear deformation caused by the singular stress field in the vicinity of the crack tip [31]. With decrease of the specimen size, the shape of the σglobal-εglobal curves changes and represents stronger nonlinearity. In particular, the σglobal-εglobal curve is significantly bent when the crack length is reduced to a = 0.7 nm and 1.4 nm. Thus, the nonlinearity is of vital importance during the nano-fracture analysis and must be considered. Then, from the perspective of micro mechanics behavior, the fracture starts with the first bond breaking at the crack tip, and complete failure of the specimen proceeds subsequently and rapidly along the zigzag direction, resulting in two fractured pieces, as shown in Fig. 3c. Therefore, the failure of GNRs in this work is a purely brittle and mode-I fracture.
