5. Conclusions
In conclusion, this paper proposes an AFM-based J-integral (JAFM) for brittle nonlinear fracture at the nanoscale. The creative points can be summarized as follows:
1) The line integral along the crack surface is considered in the J-integral for atomic structures. 2) A critical difficulty in calculating the J-integral for a discrete system, i.e., how to describe the local displacement gradient of atoms, is overcome through meshing the atomic structure by isoparametric elements. 3) The applicability and path-independency of the JAFM method are verified. The geometric nonlinearity near the crack tip is discussed
by introducing the Green strain. It reveals the failure of the infinitesimal deformation assumption when the integral region shrinks down to the atomic scale. 4) The breakdown of NLFM at the nanoscale is investigated, and the lower dimensional limit of NLFM for brittle fracture in Zigzag direction of graphene nanoribbons (GNRs) is found, i.e., aC
NL = 6.9 nm. 5) The dimension-independency of the JAFM method is confirmed. It demonstrates that JAFM is valid at all scales even below the critical dimensional limit of NLFM.
